tests/isla/traces.sh traces the floating-point forms from armv9p4-checked-fp.ir, which this diff
makes from armv9p4-checked.ir, armv9p4.ir with armv9p4-checked.diff applied, where armv9p4.ir is
the Armv9.4-A snapshot of the Sail model (isla-snapshots d8b31014).  The Sail model computes each
floating-point result by rounding a real number, which Isla does not implement, so the traces
leave the floating-point functions abstract with --abstract, and Isla makes the result of an
abstract function a fresh variable, which needs a return type of fixed width (executor.rs 950-971
of Isla e9b5d945).  FPAdd, FPSub, FPMul, FPDiv, FPSqrt, and FixedToFP return bits(N) for the width
N of their operands or their argument N, and FPToFixed returns bits(M) for its argument M.

The diff adds FPAdd64, FPSub64, FPMul64, FPDiv64, FPSqrt64, FixedToFP64, and FPToFixed64, which
take and return 64-bit vectors and call the original function, FixedToFP64 with N = 64 and
FPToFixed64 with M = 64, and a dispatcher of each, FPAdd_sized and the others, which calls the
wrapper when its first operand has 64 bits, and FixedToFP_sized when N is 64 as well and
FPToFixed_sized when M is 64 as well, and calls the original function otherwise.  The call of each
function in the execute function of FADD and FSUB (float_arithmetic_add_sub), FMUL
(float_arithmetic_mul_product), FDIV (float_arithmetic_div), FSQRT (float_arithmetic_unary), and
UCVTF and SCVTF from a general register and the conversions such as FCVTZU to a general register
(float_convert_int) serves the forms of every element size, and the diff makes it call the
dispatcher in place of the function.  An abstract wrapper
therefore stands only for the 64-bit call: FADD on S registers still calls FPAdd, whose
arithmetic on real numbers stops Isla's run with an error.  A dispatcher computes what the original
function computes for every width, so without --abstract the edit changes no trace: the traces of
the integer forms and the memory forms from the edited IR equal those from armv9p4-checked.ir, those
of LDR, STR, and STRB after the fields of each struct are sorted by name, an order that varies
between Isla's runs.

The new functions lie before FPSub_ZA, carry no source locations, and their exception paths jump
to `arbitrary`, as the IR's own functions do.  Each call site keeps its statement index, so no
jump target moves.  Apply with
`cp armv9p4-checked.ir armv9p4-checked-fp.ir && patch armv9p4-checked-fp.ir < armv9p4-checked-fp.diff`.

